## 5. Main slide

TIME 0:45. Take the Karpathy perspective included in the repository. Its documented lenses include the gap between a demo and reliable deployment, difficult tail cases, and learning from real implementations. Suppose an AI4Research agent sees a paper claiming a highly reliable research assistant. A procedure derived from those lenses would ask which task distribution the benchmark covers, what happens in a long run when tools fail, and which evidence can be reproduced. This shows how a model becomes research questions. The response is our illustration derived from the skill, not a quotation or a claim about Karpathy's view of a particular paper. These questions may also arise from a generic checklist. Added value over a generic reviewer is untested, which is why that comparator belongs in the trial.

SOURCES
https://github.com/alchaincyf/nuwa-skill/blob/7812110bf7e5a2b01b6aa5a738568044121bbfa2/examples/andrej-karpathy-perspective/SKILL.md

## 6. Main slide

TIME 0:50. There are four lessons to carry into AI4Research. A public record is incomplete, so we should keep claim-level sources and label inference. A distinctive voice can create authority, so scientific outputs should use evidence rather than a person's name as support. Familiar public-position tests measure a narrow kind of fidelity, so we need new research tasks and independent scoring. Finally, the quality checker counts structure and keywords: our saved empty-content probe passed all six functions, including missing sources. AI4R should fail checks when required evidence is missing and use semantic assessment separately from lint. Separate judging agents still need protection against shared-model biases. Nuwa itself acknowledges many limitations; the issue is whether its checks enforce them in the output.

SOURCES
